  "body": "### What is the issue with the Encoding Standard?\n\nThe [peek](https://encoding.spec.whatwg.org/#i-o-queue-peek) algorithm says, in step 3:\n\n> For each n in the range 1 to number, inclusive:\n> 1. If ioQueue[n] is end-of-queue, break.\n> 2. Otherwise, append ioQueue[n] to prefix.\n\nInfra list indexing is [zero-based](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list), so this collects `ioQueue[1]` through `ioQueue[number]`: it skips the first item and includes one item too many. Taken literally:\n\n- [UTF-8 decode](https://encoding.spec.whatwg.org/#utf-8-decode) step 1 peeks at bytes 1–3, so a leading EF BB BF is not detected as a BOM.\n- [BOM sniff](https://encoding.spec.whatwg.org/#bom-sniff) step 1 has the same problem.\n- Step 1 waits only until the queue's size is at least `number`, or the queue contains end-of-queue. If the size is exactly `number` and there is no end-of-queue, `ioQueue[number]` is out of bounds. For an empty, finished queue (just end-of-queue), `ioQueue[1]` is out of bounds. Infra says the index cannot be out-of-bounds.\n\nThe intent is clearly the first number items.\n\nSuggested fix: in step 3, replace \"the range 1 to number, inclusive\" with \"[the range](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#the-exclusive-range) 0 to number, exclusive\". That also covers `number` = 0, because Infra defines that range as empty when the bounds are equal.\n\nThe wording dates from #215.",
